    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <p xml:id="Aink429-kilavi">kuṟippiṉāl pirivu uṇarntu vēṟuppaṭṭa talaimakaḷ uṭampaṭuvāḷāka vēntaṟku uṟṟuḻip piriyum talaimakaṉ colliyatu.</p>
          <p xml:lang="en">So that the heroine who had, on understanding the [imminent] separation through [his] signs, would agree, the hero who is separating for a royal emergency spoke [this].</p>
            </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink429">        
            <l>palliruṅ kūntal pacappu nīviṭiṟ</l>
                <l>celvēn tilla yāmē ceṟṟār</l>                
                <l>velkoṭi yaraṇa murukkiya</l>               
                <l>kallā yāṉai ventupakai velaṟkē.</l></lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
<l>O one with the thick dark hair, if you let go of [your] pallor</l>
<l>I will go to be be victoious over the enemies of the king</l>
<l>whose unlearned<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">The connotations of <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kallā</foreign> are normally that a human has not been fully educated - possibly a sign of lower status or of vulgarity. Here, one could be tempted to translate with something like 'untamed'; however, that would be an undesirable trait of an elephant in the hero's king's army. Rather, I understand that suggests the elephents are brutish. I am not certain on this point, though, so I have provided a literal translation.</note> elephants have ruined</l>
<l>the forts with victory flags of those who resist.</l>
          </lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
